  "verdict": [
   "New: all 100 board names (and the #1) pass a $30K test \u2014 at most 5% of the stock's expected volume, at most 0.5% estimated price impact, an as-traded price of $1 or more and no short-sale restriction; live, each board name must also be lendable at IBKR for $30K or more whenever IBKR's list is available (otherwise borrow shows as unknown). About 1173 stocks pass the volume, impact and price test on a typical day, before the borrow, squeeze and halt checks.",
   "The honest catch: the #1 dumps far less often. In the backtest the $30K board's #1 fell 5%+ open\u2192close on 26% / 28% of sessions (old #1: 52% / 54%); ranks 51\u2013100 on 11% / 12% (old: 24% / 24%). Most of that is liquidity \u2014 the dump-odds model's own #1 among the same $30K-ready names fell 5%+ on 36% / 36% \u2014 and part is the ranker, which was chosen for all 100 names, not for the #1: its #1 dumps less often and netted no better at $30K (\u22122.0% / \u22121.6% versus \u22121.0% / \u22121.6% for the dump-odds #1). Same-or-better accuracy is not available at this size: the big intraday dumps happen in stocks too thin to take $30K.",
   "The old #1 itself, even charged its large estimated cost, netted +0.4% / +0.5% at $30K \u2014 better than the new #1's \u22122.0% / \u22121.6%. But it could take $30K within the volume and impact limits on only 24% / 18% of days \u2014 the rest of the time a $30K short was exactly the heavy slippage this change exists to avoid, and the cost model is least reliable for such oversized orders.",
   "Across all 100 at $30K a name, the new board is far better than the old one: per position after estimated costs \u22121.6% / \u22121.5%, versus \u22124.1% / \u22124.3% for the old board, whose thin names would pay about 5.0% to trade $30K.",
   "But shorting all 100 at $30K still lost money in the backtest: the average name moved only \u22120.2% open\u2192close against about 1.8% estimated cost (\u22120.9% per position if real spreads are half the assumed ones, \u22122.8% if double). The top 10 come closest (\u22121.2% / \u22121.4%; half spreads \u22120.6% / \u22120.8%). Treat the lower ranks as a watchlist.",
   "The evening watchlist, and the morning page whenever the pre-market feed check fails, use the close-only version of the ranker: its #1 fell 5%+ on 29% / 22% and netted \u22120.1% / \u22123.0%; all 100 netted \u22121.7% / \u22121.6% per position. The morning version above re-scores the top 450 $30K-ready names with the pre-market gap \u2014 exactly as the live run does.",
   "Six ranking models were compared under a rule written down before the run: rank by development 100-name net, treat gaps under 0.05 pt as ties, break ties by development top-10 net, then require confirmation no worse than the production model. It picked a regression on its within-day percentile. The six spanned \u22121.5% to \u22121.7% per position; a dump classifier trained only on these names, a 3%-drop classifier and an average of three of them were measurably worse than production in at least one window (paired 90% interval below zero); the rest were statistically indistinguishable from production; against production, the adopted ranker's paired 90% interval spans zero in both windows. A second pre-registered step tested adding counts of SEC filings accepted overnight (up to 8:00 ET). Its first run took filing histories only for today's \u2264 $2B universe \u2014 a look-ahead (names that later grew had none), caught in review \u2014 so it was re-run with every stock's history: +0.01 pt / +0.05 pt per position against a bar of +0.05 pt in both windows, so overnight filings are NOT used.",
